.TH SOS_COMPARE 1 "Thu May 07 2026"
.SH NAME
sos_compare \- Compare baseline snapshots to detect system changes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sos compare
          [list] [\-\-name NAME]\fR
          [diff] <id1> <id2> [<id3> ...] [OPTIONS]\fR
.LP
\fBsos diff\fR is an alias for \fBsos compare\fR.

.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBsos compare\fR is an sos subcommand taht compares two or more baseline
snapshots previously generated by \fBsos report \-\-baseline\fR. It identifies
files that have been added, removed, or modified between snpshots, including
changes to permissions, ownership, size, timestamps, SELinux context, and
content hashes.
.LP
Baseline snapshots are stored as JSON files under
\fB/etc/sos/.baselines/\fR. Each snapshot captures file metadata collected
during an \fBsos report\fR run. By comparing snapshots from different dates,
administrators can track configuration drift, detect unauthorized changes,
or verify that expected modifications were applied.
.LP
When comparing snapshots from different hosts, a warning is emitted to
alert the user that differences may be due to host variance rather than
configuration drift.

.SH SUBCOMMANDS
The action is optional. When identifiers are given the default action is
\fBdiff\fR, otherwise it is \fBlist\fR. So \fBsos compare <id1> <id2>\fR is
equivalent to \fBsos compare diff <id1> <id2>\fR, and \fBsos compare\fR with
no arguments lists snapshots. The explicit \fBlist\fR and \fBdiff\fR keywords
are still accepted.
.TP
.B list
List all available baseline snapshots. Snapshots are displayed in
most-recently-modified order, along with their file size, on-disk
location, and collection type (full or incremental).

Use \fB\-\-name\fR to filter snapshots by baseline name.
.TP
.B [diff] <id1> <id2> [<id3> ...]
Compare two or more baseline snapshots and report differences. Each
identifier may bea date string, a baseline name, or a full path to a
snapshot file. Output is formatted as a columnar table with one column
per snapshot.

Text output is limited to three snapshots: each additional snapshot adds
a column, and more than three would exceed the terminal width, so the
\fBdiff\fR action reports an error if given four or more identifiers in
text mode. Use \fB\-\-output\-format json\fR to compare more than three
snapshots; the JSON output has no such limit.

If an identifier partialy matches multiple snapshots, the most recent
match is used.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-name NAME
Filter snapshots by baseline name when using the \fBlist\fR action.
.TP
.B \-\-output\-format {text|json}
Set the output format for the \fBdiff\fR action. The default is
\fBtext\fR, which provides a human-readable columnar table of changes.
Use \fBjson\fR for machine-parseable output suitable for further
processing or integration with monitoring tools.

Default: text
.TP
.B \-p, \-\-profile, \-\-profiles PROFILE
Only compare files from plugins belonging to the specified profile.
Profiles are a semantic grouping used by sos plugins (e.g. \fBkernel\fR,
\fBnetwork\fR, \fBsystem\fR, \fBhardware\fR, \fBservices\fR). This
option can be specified multiple times to include files from multiple
profiles. Profile membership is recorded per-plugin in the snapshot
manifest.

Example: \fB\-\-profile kernel\fR shows only differences from plugins
that belong to the kernel profile (e.g. kernel, modules).
.TP
.B \-i, \-\-include PATTERN
Only include file paths matching the given glob pattern. This option
can be specified multiple times. Patterns are matched against the full
source path using shell-style wildcards (fnmatch).

Example: \fB\-i '/etc/*'\fR shows only differences for files under /etc.
.TP
.B \-e, \-\-exclude PATTERN
Exclude file paths matching the given glob pattern. This option can be
specified multiple times. Exclusions are applied after inclusions.

Example: \fB\-e '/proc/*'\fR hides all /proc files from the comparison.

.SH FILTERING
Filtering options can be combined to narrow comparison output:
.LP
.RS
.B Semantic filtering
(\fB\-\-profile\fR) selects plugins by topic. For example,
\fB\-\-profile kernel\fR includes files from all plugins that declare
membership in the kernel profile, regardless of their name.
.LP
.B Path filtering
(\fB\-i\fR/\fB\-e\fR) selects or excludes individual file paths using
glob patterns. Include patterns are applied first; exclude patterns
remove matches from the included set.
.LP
Both layers compose: \fB\-\-profile kernel \-e '/proc/*'\fR shows
kernel-related files excluding anything under /proc.
.RE

.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
List all available bsaeline snapshots:
.B sos compare list
.TP
List only snapshots named "production":
.B sos compare list \-\-name production
.TP
Compare two snapshots by date:
.B sos compare diff 2026-07-01_10-00-00 2026-07-03_10-00-00
.TP
Compare three snapshots side by side:
.B sos compare diff snap1 snap2 snap3
.TP
Compare with JSON output:
.B sos compare diff snap1 snap2 \-\-output\-format json
.TP
Show only kernel-related differences:
.B sos compare diff snap1 snap2 \-\-profile kernel
.TP
Show only /etc changes, excluding /etc/alternatives:
.B sos compare diff snap1 snap2 \-i '/etc/*' \-e '/etc/alternatives/*'
.TP
Combine profile and path filtering:
.B sos compare diff snap1 snap2 \-\-profile network \-e '/proc/*'
.TP
Compare a snapshot against a specific file by full path:
.B sos compare diff /etc/sos/.baselines/baseline-web01-2026-07-01_10-00-00Z.json production
.TP
Detect drift and act on the exit in a script (JSON + jq):
.B sos compare diff snap1 snap2 \-\-output\-format json | jq \-e '.summary.changed_files == 0'
.TP
Compare snapshots from a custom directory:
.B sos compare diff snap1 snap2 \-\-baseline\-dir /var/lib/baselines

.SH SAMPLE OUTPUT
The \fBdiff\fR action renders a columnar table, one column per snapshot,
listing only files and fields that differ. A \fB\-\fR
marks a field or file absent from that snapshot:
.LP
.nf
.RS
$ sos compare diff 2026\-07\-01_10\-00\-00 2026\-07\-03_10\-00\-00
┌───────────┬─────────────────────┬─────────────────────┐
│ Field     │ 2026\-07\-01_10\-00\-00 │ 2026\-07\-03_10\-00\-00 │
╞═══════════╪═════════════════════╪═════════════════════╡
│ >> /etc/ssh/sshd_config                               │
├───────────┼─────────────────────┼─────────────────────┤
│   size    │ 3218                │ 3301                │
│   mode    │ 0600                │ 0644                │
│   sha256  │ 6f1c9a2             │ 9b30e71             │
╞═══════════╪═════════════════════╪═════════════════════╡
│ >> /etc/cron.d/backup                                 │
├───────────┼─────────────────────┼─────────────────────┤
│   _status │ -                   │ present             │
│   size    │ -                   │ 142                 │
└───────────┴─────────────────────┴─────────────────────┘
 2 changed, 1487 unchanged, 1489 total
.RE
.fi
.LP
With \fB\-\-output\-format json\fR the same result is emitted as a
machine\-parseable object, suitable for piping to tools such as \fBjq\fR:
.LP
.nf
.RS
$ sos compare diff snap1 snap2 \-\-output\-format json | jq '.summary'
{
  "total_files": 1489,
  "changed_files": 2,
  "unchanged_files": 1487
}
.RE
.fi

.SH BASELINE WORKFLOW
To use \fBsos compare\fR, first generate baseline snapshots using
\fBsos report\fR:
.LP
.RS
1. Capture an initial baseline:
.B sos report \-\-baseline
.LP
2. After some time or system changes, capture another:
.B sos report \-\-baseline
.LP
3. Compare the two snapshots:
.B sos compare list
.B sos compare diff <date1> <date2>
.RE
.LP
Named baselines can be used to organize snapshots by environment or purpose:
.LP
.RS
.B sos report \-\-baseline \-\-baseline\-name production
.B sos compare diff production-2026-07-01 production-2026-07-03
.RE
.LP
For incremental reports that skip unchanged files, combine with \fB\-\-incremental\fR:
.LP
.RS
.B sos report \-\-baseline \-\-incremental
.RE
.LP
Baseline capture can be automated so that drift is tracked continuously.
For example, a weekly incremental baseline via \fB/etc/cron.d/sos\-baseline\fR:
.LP
.nf
.RS
# Capture a named incremental baseline every Monday at 03:00
0 3 * * 1 root /usr/sbin/sos report \-\-batch \-\-baseline \-\-incremental \-\-baseline\-name weekly
.RE
.fi
.LP
Run \fBsos compare list \-\-name weekly\fR to review the captures, then
compare the two most recent by passing their full paths:
.LP
.nf
.RS
recent=$(ls \-t /etc/sos/.baselines/baseline\-*weekly*.json | head \-n 2)
sos compare diff $recent
.RE
.fi

.SH SNAPSHOT CHAIN
Each baseline snapshot records its collection type (\fBfull\fR or
\fBincremental\fR) and system identity (hostname, kernel, architecture)
in its manifest. Incremental snapshots also record a reference to
the previous snapshot they were based on, including its filename,
creation time, and collection type. This chain can be followed to
reconstruct the full history of snapshots on a system.

.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/sos/.baselines/
Default directory for baseline snapshot storage. Snapshots are saved as
\fBbaseline[\-HOSTNAME][\-NAME]\-YYYY\-MM\-DD_HH\-MM\-SSZ.json\fR with
read-only permissions (0400). The directory is created with mode 0700
if it does not exist.
.LP
Snapshots stored here are the local, un-obfuscated baseline used for
incremental comparison and are \fBnever\fR included in a report archive,
even with \fB\-\-clean\fR; only the obfuscated \fBprevious_baseline.json\fR
embedded in an incremental report is cleaned. Because they hold raw host
inventory (source paths, hostname, owners), the directory is kept
root-only.

